Hand 6 TO 5
https://tinyurl.com/2yudracf
qx|A5|st||rh||ah|Board 5 IMPs   -   On November 20, 2024|pn|hondo717,vs_bbo,marikaSz,aspiring|
md|4SK973HK3DJT62CKJ9,SAT865H9DAQ74C876,SQHAQ7652DK53CQ42,SJ42HJT84D98CAT53|sv|e|
mb|P|MB|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I have another hand that is not as good as it looks. The hand is flat 11 count, although this hand is not as bad with lots of tens and nines. Regardless, it does not meet the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=ruleof20,Rule of 20^*N|CP||FP||at| (11 HCP + 8 cards in d/s = 19). So I will not open.|
PG| ***** 81 ***** |


mb|P|MB|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Many players with West's hand would open 1@S in 3rd seat. I am not a big believer in opening light in 3rd seat. I prefer the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=ruleof20,Rule of 20^*N|CP||FP||at| even in 3rd and 4th seat, although I am in the minority here.|
PG| ***** 82 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Here, I still open the hand because all of the honors are in the two long suits, and partner is a passed hand. The only question is whether to open 1@S or 2@S. Sometimes, in 3rd seat, since partner has passed, you stretch to open 2@S, even with only a 5-card suit, if your hand is unbalanced. However on this hand, because my 5-card spade suit is relatively weak, I prefer to open 1@S.|
PG| ***** 83 ***** |


mb|1S|mb|2H|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|2S|MB|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|2@H does not promise opening strength. My @SK is probably not worth much on offense. I could bid 2N, but if partner is weak that is dangerous. So I pass.|
PG| ***** 84 ***** |


mb|P|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|3H|sk|s|mb|P|MB|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Although far from obvious I know partner is short in spades since I have 4 and they have at least 8. Now I am wondering about bidding 3N. It is likely partner has AQxxxxx in hearts. Actually better than that since partner bid 3@H. So on a spade lead in 3NT we have 7 tricks at least--6 hearts and a spade. The question is can I find 2 more tricks.|
PG| ***** 85 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If partner has the @CA or the @DAK I can. But that is very specific. So I will pass and not punish partner for competing, especially at this vulnerability where we are not and they are meaning partner was more aggressive. But I wouldn't be shocked if we can make 4@H or 3N.|
PG| ***** 86 ***** |


mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 87 ***** |


sk||nt|^z@3^b@2^^|lb|*CLead for East by clicking that card on the right.^^S2^SJ42HJT84CAT53D98|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The correct lead is a low spade. Leading @SJ promises the 10. Leading a club can not possibly be right. Leading a diamond is possible. But either a heart or the @DA could be okay.|
PG| ***** 89 ***** |


